> 8.14a The object is REFUSED, and is not measured, if either (i) `F > 819`, that is more than 5% of the raster is flagged, or (ii) any flagged pixel lies inside `T`. Condition (i) is stated in OUTPUT-pixel units. V38 states exactly what is frozen: `F` counts the §8.9d NEAREST-NEIGHBOUR predicate only — an output pixel is flagged iff its own nearest-neighbour source pixel was rejected. Bilinear IMAGE values can also draw, through the 2-by-2 stencil, on a replaced source pixel whose nearest-neighbour flag lies in a different output pixel — including inside `T` when that flag is outside `T` (codex V37, executed with a fractional WCS offset). `F` does NOT count every output value influenced by replacement, and `T` protects against FLAGGED pixels, not against every stencil contribution. That is the identity the signed option A rule V15 §6 froze and the one this amendment keeps; the cost is disclosed here rather than promised away. A full-stencil predicate would be a design change requiring its own gate and approval and is NOT made here. Condition (ii) replaces the fixed central aperture of an earlier refused draft, which did not protect a galaxy larger than 32 by 32 pixels: a contaminant on the outer arms of a large galaxy would have been replaced by a background median, carving a hole in the object and altering the very morphology the instrument measures. `T` scales with the object.
